### 🐛 Describe the bug

With the `slo*` routing strategies, the gateway queues requests in `queueRouter`. `queueRouter.Route()` enqueues a `QueueEntry` that points at the request's pooled `RoutingContext`, then waits in `ctx.TargetPod()` until a pod is set or the context is done ([queue_router.go](https://github.com/vllm-project/aibrix/blob/9556b62/pkg/plugins/gateway/algorithms/queue_router.go#L81-L122)).

If the client cancels while the request is queued, `TargetPod()` returns, `Route()` returns the cancellation error, and `Process` returns the routing context to the pool ([gateway.go](https://github.com/vllm-project/aibrix/blob/9556b62/pkg/plugins/gateway/gateway.go#L435-L441)). The queue entry stays queued, and nothing checks whether its requester is still waiting:
- `SLOQueue.Peek()` ranks the entry from `entry.RoutingContext` and routes it with `subRoute()` ([slo_queue.go](https://github.com/vllm-project/aibrix/blob/9556b62/pkg/plugins/gateway/queue/slo_queue.go#L221-L348)).
- `queueRouter.routeNext()` calls the backend router and `AddRequestCount` with `entry.RoutingContext` ([queue_router.go](https://github.com/vllm-project/aibrix/blob/9556b62/pkg/plugins/gateway/algorithms/queue_router.go#L170-L224)).

If another request has already taken that context from the pool, these calls act on the new request instead: it is routed out of turn, through the canceled request's queue entry, before its own entry is ranked. If the context has not been recycled yet, the queue still spends routing work and request counts on a client that is gone.

This happens when the queue is backed up and clients time out, which is the overload case that #3189 describes.

### Steps to Reproduce

The Go test below, placed in `pkg/plugins/gateway/algorithms`, holds the serve goroutine at its first `Peek`, cancels the queued request, recycles its routing context as `req-next` with `types.RecycleRoutingContextForTest` (as the pool does), then lets the queue run. A recording backend router logs the request ID of each context it routes.

```
go test ./pkg/plugins/gateway/algorithms/ -run TestQueueSkipsRequestCanceledWhileQueued -count=1
```

Output on main (9556b62) with the test added:

```
--- FAIL: TestQueueSkipsRequestCanceledWhileQueued (0.01s)
    queue_router_cancel_test.go:115: backend routed [req-next], want nothing: the canceled request was routed
```

The canceled request's entry was routed, and the router received the context that now belongs to `req-next`.

